  "body": "The 4.39.0 Providers page was rejected, and correctly. It put every provider behind a collapsed category group, so reaching one meant **section → closed group → card → closed \"Advanced\"**: four levels, two shut by default, and a page that opened showing mostly headings. It solved \"too much scrolling\" by *hiding things*, which is a worse problem.\n\n## Categories are gone\n\n\"Inference platform\" vs \"Gateway\" is a way of thinking about the vendor landscape, not information anyone needs in order to paste a key. Grouping by it made finding a provider harder. The dot rail went with it — it was an abstraction you had to learn before it told you anything.\n\n## What comes back, and what actually gets fixed\n\nThe original **240px card grid** and the original **status pill** return unchanged.\n\nThe one structural change is the thing that was genuinely wrong before: a provider's **status** and its **key fields** lived in two different places on the page. They are now the same card. Press **Configure** → the card expands across the row to hold the whole provider form. Press **Done** → it collapses.\n\n**Refresh models now sits on the card face next to Configure**, reachable without opening anything. It was buried inside the expanded form. Local providers show \"Test connection\" in the same slot.\n\n## The form is the whole provider\n\nThis is what earlier iterations of this work misrepresented. A provider is not a name and a key — it is a small form:\n\n- the **multi-key pool**, each key with health, plus add and remove\n- the **rotation policy** select (single / round robin / least used / failover)\n- proxy and base URL\n- the model refresh\n\nNone of this was ever broken — `renderField()` attaches the key manager for a credential field, so it came along — but no version of the design *acknowledged* it, which is why the flat-grid ideas kept failing. All 35 forms cannot be open at once. That is the real constraint the layout has to respect.\n\n## New payload field\n\n`provider_status[].key_count` carries the size of each key pool so a card face can read **\"3 keys · Round robin\"** without one HTTP request per provider on load. It cannot be derived client-side: secrets are masked to a constant before they leave the server. The rotation label comes from the `_ROTATION` field already in the payload.\n\n## Verification\n\nExecuted in jsdom against the real 296-field payload. This harness checks the things the previous one **excluded and therefore could not have caught**:\n\n```\nPASS  provider cards render                          35 cards\nPASS  no accordion groups remain                     0 found\nPASS  EVERY provider field incl. _ROTATION in DOM    107 fields\nPASS  rotation selects present                       31/31\nPASS  key managers present                           31 managers\nPASS  Configure + Refresh models on the face         Configure | Refresh models\nPASS  summary line shows the key pool                \"3 keys · Round robin\"\nPASS  local provider offers Test connection          Configure | Test connection\nPASS  Configure opens the card                       label now \"Done\"\nPASS  open card exposes key manager + rotation       both inside the card\nPASS  key pool lists keys with health + Remove       3 rows, 3 Remove buttons\nPASS  filtering detaches nothing                     107 still attached; 4 visible\nPASS  a filtered-out card does not stay open         collapsed when filtered away\nPASS  count line reads correctly                     \"35 providers · 2 configured\"\nPASS  all admin views render                         7 views\nPASS  no uncaught script errors                      clean\n\n16/16\n```\n\n```\nruff format / ruff check / ty: clean\npytest: 3313 passed, 6 failed\n```\n\nThe 6 are the documented PowerShell installer baseline, identical on unmodified `main` in the same shell.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **Still not rendered in a browser.** No browser tooling is reachable in this session. jsdom proves structure and wiring, not appearance.\n- An expanded card takes the full grid row (`grid-column: 1 / -1`), so opening one reflows the cards after it. Deliberate — the key pool needs the width — but it is the most likely thing to feel wrong in practice.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
